const DATA={"summary": [{"title": "1. The central idea: working software is not automatically responsible software", "slides": "1–6, 39–42", "lead": "This lecture shifts the engineer’s attention from technical feasibility to ethical consequences and wider social value.", "blocks": [["Three different engineering questions", "<strong>Can we build it?<\/strong> tests feasibility, technology and resources. <strong>Should we build it?<\/strong> tests whether the system has a justified purpose, meets a real need, and raises ethical concerns. <strong>What could happen?<\/strong> examines benefits, risks and unintended effects. A responsible engineer must answer all three; technical success answers only the first."], ["Why software ethics matters", "Software shapes education, healthcare, banking, employment, transportation, public services, communication and everyday decisions. A flawed decision in any of these settings may harm people who never wrote, purchased or directly used the software. Therefore, ethical evaluation must be part of design and deployment, not an afterthought."], ["The 92% AI student-risk problem", "A university’s AI tool predicts which students might drop out and reports <strong>92% accuracy<\/strong>. That metric does not tell us whether individual students have been mislabelled, whether some groups are treated unfairly, or whether sensitive data is handled responsibly. False positives could cause anxiety or affect lecturers’ expectations. Students may have no effective way to challenge a label. The defensible position is <strong>conditional deployment only after a stakeholder, fairness, privacy and safety review<\/strong>; accuracy alone is not permission to deploy."], ["An exam-ready thesis", "A software system can satisfy its functional requirements while violating privacy, reinforcing bias, excluding users or creating unintended harm. Engineers are responsible for evaluating both <strong>how well a system works<\/strong> and <strong>what happens because it works<\/strong>."]]}, {"title": "2. Ethics, law and professional responsibility", "slides": "7–10", "lead": "Ethics means reasoned judgement about responsible, fair and appropriate actions when decisions affect others.", "blocks": [["Six ethical considerations", "<strong>Harm and benefit:<\/strong> who gains, and who might suffer? <strong>Fairness:<\/strong> does the system treat people unjustly or exclude some groups? <strong>Privacy:<\/strong> are personal data collected and used responsibly? <strong>Safety:<\/strong> could a failure create physical, financial or emotional harm? <strong>Honesty:<\/strong> are abilities, limitations and risks represented truthfully? <strong>Accountability:<\/strong> who explains, corrects and takes responsibility for a bad outcome?"], ["Ethics ≠ law ≠ professional expectations", "The slide distinguishes <strong>legal<\/strong> requirements (what law permits or requires), <strong>professional<\/strong> expectations (standards of professional conduct), and <strong>ethical<\/strong> judgement (what responsible action requires). Company policy can add workplace rules but cannot, by itself, settle what is morally responsible. A practice might be permitted or authorised internally and still expose users to unacceptable risk."], ["The “I Agree” scenario", "An app asks for location, contacts, microphone access and usage data. A user tapping “I Agree” does not automatically discharge an engineer’s obligations. The engineer should question whether each permission is necessary, whether the request is understandable, who could be affected and whether users have meaningful alternatives. <em>Suggested application beyond the slide:<\/em> request permissions only when needed, clearly explain purposes and allow reasonable user control."], ["Why professional codes exist", "A professional code provides a set of principles for judgement when technical, business and social interests conflict. The lecture uses the <strong>ACM/IEEE Software Engineering Code of Ethics and Professional Practice<\/strong>, which presents eight areas of responsibility. It guides decisions; it is not a substitute for reasoning about the specific case."]]}, {"title": "3. The eight ACM/IEEE areas of responsibility", "slides": "10–19", "lead": "Remember the lecture’s order: Public, Client & Employer, Product, Judgment, Management, Profession, Colleagues, Self.", "blocks": [["1. Public", "Public interest comes first. Consider the people beyond the paying customer, people at risk of exclusion, consequences of system failure, and the honesty of risk communication. <strong>Example:<\/strong> avoid releasing a student-scoring model that causes foreseeable harm merely because university management is satisfied."], ["2. Client & Employer", "Serve the client and employer responsibly, but <strong>consistently with public interest<\/strong>. Protect confidential information, be truthful about delivery, and work within competence and authorisation. <strong>Example:<\/strong> challenge a client’s request to secretly collect unnecessary data for future marketing."], ["3. Product", "Deliver products fit for purpose, safe, reliable and appropriately secure. Passing a test suite is not enough if limitations are unknown, the system is used in the wrong context, or users cannot recover from errors. <strong>Example:<\/strong> test how a system behaves when an incorrect automated decision is made and corrected."], ["4. Judgment", "Maintain objective, independent professional judgement when interests conflict. <strong>Example:<\/strong> if a serious security vulnerability is found one day before release, assess severity and communicate risks rather than accepting “we will fix it later” without question."], ["5. Management", "Leaders should manage development and maintenance ethically: realistic estimates, fair workload, candid risk reporting, and sufficient time for safety, security and quality. <strong>Example:<\/strong> do not conceal a delayed project by skipping testing and claiming it is on track."], ["6. Profession", "Protect the integrity and reputation of software engineering. Communicate limits honestly, speak up about unethical conduct, avoid misleading claims and share knowledge. <strong>Example:<\/strong> do not endorse “100% secure” or “bias-free” when known limitations exist."], ["7. Colleagues", "Treat colleagues fairly and supportively. Give credit, exchange constructive feedback, promote learning and well-being, and avoid public blame. <strong>Example:<\/strong> when a junior developer’s bug also escaped code review, investigate the shared process rather than publicly scapegoating one person."], ["8. Self", "Maintain competence through lifelong learning, recognise your limits, learn from mistakes and seek help when necessary. <strong>Example:<\/strong> a developer unfamiliar with AI diagnosis should not independently promise a high-stakes medical feature under an unrealistic deadline."], ["Exam distinction", "The same situation can involve <strong>several<\/strong> areas. A last-minute security flaw may engage Product, Judgment, Public and Management. In an essay, identify the primary principle, then explain the related principles instead of assuming only one applies."]]}, {"title": "4. Ethical trade-offs and an explicit decision check", "slides": "20–22", "lead": "Real-world dilemmas often involve competing legitimate goals, so good answers describe the tension and justify a mitigation.", "blocks": [["Five trade-offs", "<strong>Speed ↔ safety:<\/strong> delivering quickly can reduce time available for testing. <strong>Cost ↔ quality:<\/strong> tight budgets can pressure teams to compromise reliability. <strong>Convenience ↔ privacy:<\/strong> effortless services may demand more personal information. <strong>Personalisation ↔ data collection:<\/strong> tailored results may require sensitive data. <strong>Automation ↔ human control:<\/strong> automated decisions may reduce the ability of a person to understand or contest an outcome."], ["The seven-question ethical decision check", "Ask: <strong>(1)<\/strong> Who could be affected? <strong>(2)<\/strong> What could go wrong? <strong>(3)<\/strong> Who benefits? <strong>(4)<\/strong> Who carries the risk? <strong>(5)<\/strong> Do users understand what is happening? <strong>(6)<\/strong> Is there a safer or fairer alternative? <strong>(7)<\/strong> Can the decision be explained and defended?"], ["Applying it to facial-recognition attendance", "The benefit is faster automated attendance. The concern is the collection and potential misuse of biometric data, including possible incorrect matches. Students and lecturers are stakeholders; students who cannot or do not want to use facial recognition may be affected. The engineer should ask whether an equally workable, less intrusive attendance method exists. <em>The lecture presents the case as an activity; these are worked example answers, not text quoted from the slide.<\/em>"]]}, {"title": "5. Social responsibility, ripple effects and invisible stakeholders", "slides": "23–31", "lead": "Ethics asks what action is responsible; social responsibility asks how the system affects people, communities and society.", "blocks": [["The ripple effect", "The lecture’s sequence is <strong>Software solution → Direct users → Organisation → Other stakeholders → Community → Society/environment<\/strong>. An efficiency gain for a direct user can still shift costs to other groups. Consider immediate, indirect and long-term effects."], ["Invisible stakeholders", "People need not use a system to be affected by it. The lecture names family members, employees, non-users, vulnerable groups, communities, future users and the environment. In an answer, identify at least one “invisible” group and explain the mechanism of impact, not just its name."], ["Case: fully digital government service", "Moving a service entirely online may improve convenience and speed. It may also exclude people with weak connectivity, low digital literacy, disabilities or accessibility requirements, unsuitable devices, or language/usability barriers. Digital transformation is <strong>not automatically social progress<\/strong>. Progress depends on whether improvements are accessible and fairly distributed. <em>Suggested mitigation:<\/em> retain an assisted or offline pathway, design inclusively, test accessibility and support multiple languages."], ["Stakeholder impact map", "Work through the lecture’s five prompts: <strong>Who benefits? Who may be harmed? What could go wrong? Who is accountable? How can negative impact be reduced?<\/strong> For a case question, list stakeholder + benefit + concern + responsibility, then finish with a justified decision."], ["Positive software impact", "Responsible software may improve accessibility, healthcare, educational access, disaster response, resource efficiency, financial inclusion and community services. A strong exam response states a <strong>mechanism<\/strong>: for instance, an accessible digital learning platform can remove a specific barrier to education instead of merely claiming it “supports society”."]]}, {"title": "6. SDGs: explain an actual contribution mechanism", "slides": "31–34", "lead": "The UN Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) are 17 global goals within the 2030 Agenda. The lecture highlights seven especially relevant to software.", "blocks": [["Relevant SDGs", "<strong>SDG 3:<\/strong> Good Health and Well-being. <strong>SDG 4:<\/strong> Quality Education. <strong>SDG 9:<\/strong> Industry, Innovation and Infrastructure. <strong>SDG 10:<\/strong> Reduced Inequalities. <strong>SDG 11:<\/strong> Sustainable Cities and Communities. <strong>SDG 12:<\/strong> Responsible Consumption and Production. <strong>SDG 13:<\/strong> Climate Action."], ["Case: smart waste management", "The proposed system uses IoT sensors to monitor bin capacity, predicts collection demand, optimises collection routes, offers recycling information and analyses waste patterns. It is more than a technology demo because it can target the social and environmental problem of inefficient waste management."], ["Connect actions to SDGs", "<strong>SDG 11:<\/strong> better waste collection can improve the cleanliness and management of communities. <strong>SDG 12:<\/strong> recycling information and waste-pattern analysis can support more responsible consumption and resource use. <strong>SDG 13:<\/strong> more efficient collection routes may reduce fuel use and emissions. These are <strong>plausible mechanisms, not proven outcomes<\/strong>. The lecture explicitly asks, “What exactly is the mechanism of contribution?”"], ["Evidence matters", "Do not simply attach an SDG logo to a project. Specify a problem, the software function addressing it, the expected result and what evidence would show improvement. <em>Example metrics beyond the slide:<\/em> fewer unnecessary trips, better bin-servicing coverage or higher recycling participation, measured before and after deployment."]]}, {"title": "7. Malaysian priorities: MySTIE, MyDIGITAL and Industry4WRD", "slides": "35–38", "lead": "Link each framework to its purpose without inventing details missing from the lecture.", "blocks": [["MySTIE", "The <strong>10-10 MySTIE Framework<\/strong> links <strong>10 key socioeconomic drivers<\/strong> with <strong>10 science and technology drivers<\/strong>. The lecture’s central message is to align science, technology and innovation with Malaysia’s socioeconomic needs and opportunities. It does <em>not<\/em> list the 20 drivers, so do not memorise an invented driver list as if it came from these slides."], ["MyDIGITAL", "The <strong>Malaysia Digital Economy Blueprint (MyDIGITAL)<\/strong> is presented as a national digital-economy context for software. The lecture names seven broad themes: <strong>digital services, digital adoption, data, cybersecurity, digital talent, inclusion and innovation<\/strong>. A digital public service, for instance, should support adoption and inclusion while addressing cybersecurity."], ["Industry4WRD", "The lecture identifies <strong>Industry4WRD<\/strong> as a Malaysian priority under the heading “Software Meets Industry”, but does not supply a detailed description of its initiatives, components or mechanisms. For a source-grounded exam answer, describe it cautiously as the <strong>industry-oriented national context<\/strong> named in the lecture, and relate a relevant industrial software solution to responsible innovation. Avoid attributing a list of technologies or specific targets to this slide."], ["Four perspectives — one solution", "For any case, write four short analyses: <strong>Ethics:<\/strong> is the solution responsible? <strong>Social responsibility:<\/strong> who is affected? <strong>SDGs:<\/strong> what societal problem does it address? <strong>Malaysia:<\/strong> how does it connect with MySTIE, MyDIGITAL and, where relevant, Industry4WRD? This structure follows the lecture’s final synthesis."]]}, {"title": "8. A structured-answer method for the exam", "slides": "21, 29–31, 38–42", "lead": "Turn slide keywords into complete, defensible sentences: a claim, an explanation, a consequence and a justified action.", "blocks": [["The C-E-E-A paragraph", "<strong>Claim:<\/strong> identify the ethical principle or stakeholder issue. <strong>Explain:<\/strong> say why it applies. <strong>Example/effect:<\/strong> connect it to the scenario and describe the consequences. <strong>Action:<\/strong> propose a realistic safeguard or responsible decision. For example: “Privacy is a concern because the app collects contact details that are not required for its core function. This exposes non-users’ information as well as users’. The team should remove unnecessary permissions and explain any remaining collection clearly.”"], ["Answering “evaluate / would you deploy?”", "Make a decision that matches the evidence. Then identify (1) beneficiaries and affected groups, (2) important harms, (3) relevant professional responsibilities, (4) mitigations and how to verify them, and (5) a conclusion recognising remaining limitations. The conclusion may be conditional rather than an absolute yes/no."], ["Answering “propose alignment”", "Use this chain: <strong>real-world problem → concrete software function → directly affected stakeholders → social benefit/risk → SDG → relevant Malaysian framework → evidence or safeguard<\/strong>. Avoid unsupported claims that software automatically solves national problems."], ["The three closing takeaways", "<strong>Build it right:<\/strong> technical quality, safety, security and reliability. <strong>Build the right thing:<\/strong> appropriate purpose, users, stakeholders and consequences. <strong>Think beyond the system:<\/strong> society, sustainability and the wider context. These are the lecture’s three explicit takeaways and a concise ending for longer essays."]]}], "essays": [{"topic": "Foundations", "kind": "Analyse", "marks": 10, "slides": "3–5", "question": "A university AI predicts at-risk students with 92% accuracy. Analyse why technical accuracy alone is insufficient to justify deployment.", "points": ["Separate technical performance from ethical acceptability", "Sensitive data and privacy", "False positives, stigmatisation and lecturer treatment", "Fairness and right to challenge results", "Conditional deployment with safeguards"], "answer": "A system achieving 92% accuracy may be technically promising, but its overall accuracy does not prove that its decisions are fair, harmless or appropriate for real students. The university must evaluate what happens when the system produces incorrect predictions and how those predictions influence the people who receive them.\n\nFor example, a false positive could wrongly label a student as likely to drop out. If a lecturer sees that label, it may change how the student is treated, even though the student is performing well. The student could also experience anxiety or stigma. Because the model may use sensitive student information, privacy and responsible data collection are essential. Students should understand how results are used and have a meaningful way to question incorrect classifications.\n\nTherefore, I would not deploy the system solely on the basis of accuracy. I would require checks for fairness, privacy, harmful consequences and accountability, followed by safeguards and human review before responsible deployment."}, {"topic": "Foundations", "kind": "Explain", "marks": 8, "slides": "1, 5–6", "question": "Distinguish “Can we build it?”, “Should we build it?” and “What could happen?” using a software engineering example.", "points": ["Feasibility, technology and resources", "Purpose, need and ethical appropriateness", "Benefits, risks and unintended consequences", "One integrated example"], "answer": "“Can we build it?” concerns technical feasibility: whether the available technology, resources and expertise are sufficient to create a system. “Should we build it?” considers whether the solution serves a justified purpose and whether its ethical concerns are acceptable. “What could happen?” considers its positive outcomes, risks and unintended effects after implementation.\n\nConsider a facial-recognition attendance application. Engineers may be capable of developing an accurate recognition model and integrating it with a university database. That establishes only that the system can be built. They must then decide whether capturing students’ facial data is necessary for attendance, especially when other methods may work. They must also evaluate what happens if a student is misidentified, excluded or uncomfortable with biometric monitoring.\n\nThe three questions should be answered together because successful implementation does not automatically mean the system should be deployed."}, {"topic": "Foundations", "kind": "Explain", "marks": 10, "slides": "6–7", "question": "Define ethics in software engineering and explain six considerations that ethical reasoning should include.", "points": ["Definition of ethics", "Harm and benefit", "Fairness", "Privacy and safety", "Honesty and accountability"], "answer": "Ethics in software engineering is the use of principles and reasoned judgement to determine what is responsible, fair and appropriate when software decisions affect others. It requires looking beyond whether the software meets technical specifications.\n\nFirst, engineers evaluate harm and benefit to understand who gains from a system and who might suffer negative outcomes. Second, fairness requires them to consider whether certain users or groups are treated unequally. Third, privacy concerns whether personal information is collected and handled responsibly. Fourth, safety requires attention to potential harm caused by failures or misuse. Fifth, honesty means communicating the system’s capabilities, limitations and known risks truthfully. Sixth, accountability means identifying who is responsible for decisions, failures and corrective action.\n\nTaken together, these principles help engineers make defensible decisions throughout software design, development and deployment, especially where users or the wider public could be harmed."}, {"topic": "Foundations", "kind": "Compare", "marks": 10, "slides": "8–9", "question": "Explain how legal requirements, professional obligations and ethical judgement differ. Apply your answer to an app that collects excessive user data.", "points": ["Legal means permitted or required by law", "Professional means expected conduct in software engineering", "Ethical means responsible judgement beyond minimum compliance", "Apply all three", "Give a defensible action"], "answer": "Legal requirements describe what relevant laws permit or require. Professional obligations describe what responsible members of software engineering are expected to do, such as protecting the public interest and acting competently. Ethical judgement asks whether the decision is fair and responsible even when the law or workplace policy does not prohibit it.\n\nSuppose a mobile application asks for microphone, location and contact access despite needing only basic user details. The team may argue that the user has accepted the permissions or that the company allows this practice. However, consent and internal authorisation do not automatically make excessive collection ethically justified. The engineer should ask whether each permission serves the product’s real purpose and whether collecting third-party contacts affects people who have never agreed to use the app.\n\nThe responsible approach is to challenge unnecessary collection, communicate the concern and recommend collecting only the information the application genuinely needs."}, {"topic": "Foundations", "kind": "Evaluate", "marks": 10, "slides": "9", "question": "A mobile app requests access to location, contacts, microphone and usage data. The user clicks “I Agree.” Evaluate whether the engineer’s ethical responsibility ends there.", "points": ["Clearly state that consent is not the end of responsibility", "Necessity and proportionality of collection", "Clarity and user understanding", "Possible impact on non-users and privacy", "Practical improvement"], "answer": "The engineer’s responsibility does not end when the user clicks “I Agree.” The lecture asks this question because a technical record of consent is not the same as an ethical justification for collecting information. The engineer must still consider the purpose of each permission, whether the request is understandable and which people may be affected.\n\nFor example, an app may legitimately need location to provide nearby services but have no clear reason to collect the user’s contacts or use the microphone. Contact collection can also affect people who are not app users. If data is gathered beyond the product’s purpose, users may lose privacy while receiving little additional benefit.\n\nTherefore, the team should examine whether each permission is essential and communicate clearly how any necessary information will be used. In practical design, unnecessary permissions should be removed and users should have meaningful control wherever possible. Responsible design requires more than obtaining agreement."}, {"topic": "Professional code", "kind": "Explain", "marks": 12, "slides": "10–11", "question": "Describe all eight areas of responsibility in the ACM/IEEE Software Engineering Code of Ethics and explain why the code matters.", "points": ["Public; Client & Employer; Product; Judgment", "Management; Profession; Colleagues; Self", "Brief explanation of each area", "Purpose when business and social interests conflict"], "answer": "The ACM/IEEE Software Engineering Code of Ethics and Professional Practice provides guidance when technical, business and social interests conflict. It identifies eight areas of responsibility.\n\nThe Public area requires engineers to consider possible harm to society. Client & Employer concerns serving those parties responsibly while remaining consistent with the public interest. Product means delivering software that is fit for purpose, safe and reliable. Judgment requires independent and objective professional decisions. Management concerns realistic schedules, fair work practices, honest reporting and adequate quality resources. Profession asks engineers to uphold the integrity and reputation of software engineering. Colleagues means offering fair treatment, appropriate credit and constructive support to fellow professionals. Self requires lifelong learning and an awareness of personal competence.\n\nThese principles matter because software work is not only a business or programming activity. An engineer may face pressure to release defective software or collect unnecessary data. The code gives a professional basis for recognising the conflict and defending a more responsible decision."}, {"topic": "Professional code", "kind": "Analyse", "marks": 10, "slides": "12–13", "question": "A client instructs an engineer to collect extra personal data secretly for future marketing. Discuss the conflict between public interest and responsibility to the client/employer.", "points": ["State client/employer duties and public-interest limit", "Identify the privacy concern", "Explain why quiet collection is inappropriate", "Propose honest discussion and alternative", "Explain the effect on trust"], "answer": "Software engineers should act in the best interests of their clients and employers, but the lecture explicitly states that this responsibility must remain consistent with the public interest. Therefore, a request from a paying client is not automatically acceptable.\n\nIn this case, secretly collecting personal data for possible future marketing creates an ethical conflict. Users could be exposed to privacy risks without understanding why the information was gathered. The collection is also beyond what the application needs, making its necessity difficult to defend. The engineer has a responsibility to be honest and to consider whether non-users or other stakeholders could also be affected.\n\nI would raise the issue with the client, explain the risks to users and organisational trust, and recommend limiting collection to data required for the current service. If the business later develops a genuine additional purpose, its data practices should be reviewed transparently. Protecting public interest supports responsible client service rather than contradicting it."}, {"topic": "Professional code", "kind": "Explain", "marks": 10, "slides": "14", "question": "Explain why “the software passed testing” is not enough to meet product responsibility.", "points": ["Fit for purpose", "Known limitations", "Safety and security", "Intended use context", "Error recovery"], "answer": "Passing a test suite is useful evidence that certain expected behaviours work, but it does not prove that the entire product is responsible. Under the ACM/IEEE Product area, engineers should evaluate whether the system is fit for purpose and whether important limitations are understood.\n\nFor example, a system may pass ordinary functional tests but still behave dangerously when users enter unexpected information or when it is deployed outside its intended context. It may also create security risks or leave users unable to recover from an incorrect action. These issues matter because the real consequences of failure may extend beyond what developers initially tested.\n\nResponsible product thinking therefore includes reviewing safety and security, considering how the system will actually be used, explaining known limitations and ensuring that errors can be corrected where appropriate. Testing is part of professional product responsibility, but not its conclusion."}, {"topic": "Professional code", "kind": "Evaluate", "marks": 10, "slides": "15", "question": "A serious security vulnerability is found one day before release. Management says, “Launch now. We will fix it later.” What should the engineer do?", "points": ["Identify relevant judgment and public/product duties", "Assess severity and potential harm", "Communicate honestly and document", "Recommend remediation, mitigation or postponement", "Justify priority over commercial pressure"], "answer": "The engineer should apply objective professional judgement rather than accept the launch instruction without assessing its consequences. The vulnerability creates potential risks for users, so the Product and Public areas of responsibility are also relevant.\n\nFirst, I would establish the vulnerability’s severity, the likelihood of exploitation and the possible impact on data, system safety or reliability. Second, I would report those findings clearly to management instead of concealing them to protect the release schedule. If the issue creates an unacceptable risk, I would recommend fixing it and repeating relevant tests before deployment. Any proposed temporary mitigation should be assessed rather than assumed to be safe.\n\nA delayed launch can create cost and scheduling problems, but those concerns do not automatically outweigh foreseeable harm to users. The engineer’s decision should be explainable, evidence-based and consistent with public interest. “We will fix it later” is not a sufficient ethical justification on its own."}, {"topic": "Professional code", "kind": "Analyse", "marks": 10, "slides": "16", "question": "A manager tells developers to skip testing and report an already-delayed project as “on track.” Analyse the ethical responsibilities of the manager and team.", "points": ["Management duties for realistic schedules", "Honest reporting", "Safety/security/quality resources", "Fair workload and pressure", "Actions by the team"], "answer": "Management responsibility requires leaders to use realistic estimates, treat their teams fairly, communicate problems honestly and allocate sufficient resources for safety, security and quality. Asking developers to skip testing and misrepresent progress conflicts with these responsibilities.\n\nIf the project is already delayed, claiming that it is “on track” misleads stakeholders who need accurate information to make decisions. Removing testing also transfers risk to users, who may experience failures that could have been found before release. The team may feel pressure to comply, but professional judgement and product responsibility still apply to individual engineers.\n\nThe manager should report the real status, identify the causes of delay and propose a revised plan that retains appropriate testing. Engineers should document technical risks and communicate them constructively. The aim is not simply to protect a timetable; it is to deliver a trustworthy product while treating staff and affected users responsibly."}, {"topic": "Professional code", "kind": "Evaluate", "marks": 8, "slides": "17", "question": "A company advertises its product as “100% secure” and “bias-free” despite known limitations. Should an engineer approve this claim?", "points": ["Do not approve unsupported absolutes", "Honesty and limitations", "Profession and public interest", "Recommend accurate wording and evidence"], "answer": "An engineer should not approve claims of “100% secure” or “bias-free” when important limitations are already known. The ACM/IEEE Profession area requires engineers to protect professional integrity and avoid misleading claims about what software can and cannot do.\n\nThe statements are problematic because customers and users may rely on them when deciding whether to trust or adopt the system. A promise of perfect security could cause organisations to underestimate remaining threats, while a claim of zero bias could hide unequal outcomes for some groups. Such advertising could damage public trust in software engineering.\n\nI would explain the known limitations to the relevant decision-makers and recommend wording that accurately describes what was tested, what safeguards exist and what risks remain. Claims should be supported by appropriate evidence. Honest communication benefits users and protects the reputation of both the product and profession."}, {"topic": "Professional code", "kind": "Apply", "marks": 8, "slides": "18", "question": "A junior developer is publicly blamed for a bug, but you know the code-review process also missed it. How should you respond?", "points": ["Colleague fairness and constructive feedback", "Recognise shared process failure", "Avoid scapegoating", "Propose review and learning"], "answer": "I would avoid adding to the public blame because the ACM/IEEE Colleagues area requires engineers to treat teammates fairly and provide constructive support. Although the junior developer wrote code containing the bug, the review process also failed to detect it, so presenting the incident as one person’s failure would be misleading.\n\nI would address the factual situation respectfully, preferably in a suitable team or private discussion, and explain that both implementation and review should be examined. The aim should be to find the cause and improve future work, such as by clarifying review checks or adding a test that catches the same defect. Feedback to the developer should be specific and helpful rather than humiliating.\n\nThis approach protects fairness, encourages learning and makes it more likely that teammates will report mistakes honestly. Public blame may discourage openness and prevent the team from correcting the underlying weakness."}, {"topic": "Professional code", "kind": "Evaluate", "marks": 10, "slides": "19", "question": "You are asked to build an AI health-diagnosis feature using a technique you have never used, under a tight deadline. Should you accept?", "points": ["Self/competence and high-stakes context", "Disclose experience honestly", "Seek expertise and realistic schedule", "Safety checks and validation", "Conditional decision or decline"], "answer": "I should not accept the task unconditionally or pretend that I am already competent in an unfamiliar technique. The ACM/IEEE Self area requires ongoing learning, awareness of personal limitations and knowing when to seek help or decline a task.\n\nA health-diagnosis feature is especially sensitive because a wrong result could influence a person’s health decisions. The tight deadline increases the risk that important limitations, testing and safety requirements will be overlooked. I would tell the project leader about my current experience and propose involving appropriately qualified specialists, allowing time for training and evaluating the system’s performance and risks.\n\nIf sufficient expertise, safeguards and a realistic plan can be provided, I could contribute under appropriate supervision while learning. If the project cannot be completed responsibly, I should recommend postponing or declining that responsibility. Admitting limits is professional judgement, not a failure to support the team."}, {"topic": "Ethical decisions", "kind": "Compare", "marks": 10, "slides": "20", "question": "Discuss the five ethical trade-offs in the lecture and illustrate any two with concrete software examples.", "points": ["Speed vs safety", "Cost vs quality", "Convenience vs privacy", "Personalisation vs data collection", "Automation vs human control", "Two well-explained examples"], "answer": "Software engineers frequently balance competing goals. The lecture identifies five trade-offs: speed versus safety, cost versus quality, convenience versus privacy, personalisation versus data collection, and automation versus human control. In each case, improving one desirable outcome may increase a risk elsewhere.\n\nFor example, a company can accelerate release by reducing testing time, but this may expose users to failures or security vulnerabilities. The engineer should examine whether the shortened process creates unacceptable harm rather than treating the deadline as the only priority. A second example is an AI tool that automatically identifies students considered at risk. Automation may help lecturers provide support sooner, but it may also reduce students’ ability to challenge an incorrect judgement.\n\nThe responsible decision is not always to reject new technology. Instead, engineers should identify the trade-off, evaluate who bears the risk and choose safeguards that preserve benefits without imposing unnecessary harm."}, {"topic": "Ethical decisions", "kind": "Apply", "marks": 12, "slides": "21–22", "question": "Use all seven questions in the lecture’s ethical decision check to assess a university facial-recognition attendance system.", "points": ["All seven named questions addressed", "Benefit and stakeholder", "Risks and risk bearer", "Understanding and alternative", "Explain defensibility"], "answer": "First, I would ask who could be affected: students, lecturers, administrators and people whose facial information is stored. Second, I would ask what could go wrong, such as incorrect recognition, inappropriate access to biometric data or students being unable to register attendance. Third, the university and lecturers may benefit through a faster attendance process. Fourth, students may carry privacy or misidentification risks even when they receive little direct benefit.\n\nFifth, I would ask whether students genuinely understand what information the system captures and how it will be used. Sixth, I would compare a less intrusive method, such as a non-biometric attendance process, especially for those who cannot use facial recognition. Seventh, I would consider whether the chosen design can be explained and defended as proportionate, fair and necessary.\n\nI would recommend proceeding only if those questions receive satisfactory answers and the university adopts safeguards for privacy, accuracy, accountability and reasonable alternatives. Convenience should not automatically outweigh students’ interests."}, {"topic": "Ethical decisions", "kind": "Analyse", "marks": 10, "slides": "22", "question": "For facial-recognition attendance, identify one main benefit, at least two ethical concerns, two stakeholders and a key question for the engineer.", "points": ["Clear operational benefit", "Two relevant ethical concerns", "Two stakeholders including indirectly affected if possible", "Ethical engineering question", "Mitigation"], "answer": "A major benefit of facial-recognition attendance is that a university could record attendance more quickly and reduce manual administrative work. However, the system creates ethical concerns because facial data are sensitive and an incorrect recognition result could misrecord a student’s attendance.\n\nStudents are directly affected because they must interact with the system and may suffer from incorrect records or intrusive data collection. Lecturers and university administrators are also affected because they rely on the records to monitor attendance. A student who cannot use the biometric system reliably may face exclusion or extra difficulties.\n\nThe engineer should ask: “Is facial recognition necessary to achieve the intended purpose, or could a less intrusive method provide similar benefits?” I would compare the alternatives, assess accuracy and privacy risks, and ensure that students have a workable way to correct mistakes. The system’s convenience does not remove the need for ethical judgement."}, {"topic": "Social responsibility", "kind": "Compare", "marks": 8, "slides": "23–24", "question": "Differentiate professional ethics from social responsibility, and explain the ripple effect of a software solution.", "points": ["Ethics asks what is responsible", "Social responsibility examines wider impacts", "Ripple from users to society/environment", "Illustrative consequence"], "answer": "Ethics in software engineering asks what decision is responsible, fair and appropriate. Social responsibility broadens that question by asking how software affects people, communities and society, including people who do not directly use the product.\n\nThe lecture illustrates this with a ripple effect: a software solution affects its direct users, then the organisation, other stakeholders, the surrounding community, and ultimately society or the environment. For example, automating a public service might help users complete tasks more quickly and reduce administrative effort for a government organisation. However, people without suitable devices or reliable internet access could be excluded even though they do not benefit from the change.\n\nTherefore, a responsible engineer should evaluate not only the immediate user experience but also indirect effects, unequal burdens and long-term consequences. Social responsibility is the wider impact dimension of making ethical software decisions."}, {"topic": "Social responsibility", "kind": "Explain", "marks": 10, "slides": "24–26", "question": "What is an “invisible stakeholder”? Identify four such stakeholders and explain why software engineers must consider them.", "points": ["Definition: affected even without system use", "Four examples from lecture", "Show the impact mechanism", "Link to responsible design"], "answer": "An invisible stakeholder is a person, group or entity that can be affected by software even without using it directly. The lecture gives examples including family members, employees, non-users, vulnerable groups, communities, future users and the environment.\n\nFamily members might be affected when an automated decision about one person changes household opportunities or responsibilities. Employees may experience changed workloads when their organisation adopts software. Vulnerable groups could face exclusion if services are designed around users with strong digital skills or suitable devices. The environment can also be affected through the resource use or operational changes associated with a digital service.\n\nEngineers must consider these stakeholders because direct users and paying clients do not represent everyone who carries risk. Analysing indirect impacts helps teams detect harm that might otherwise remain invisible and select more inclusive and responsible designs."}, {"topic": "Social responsibility", "kind": "Evaluate", "marks": 10, "slides": "27", "question": "A government service becomes 100% digital. Is this automatically social progress? Evaluate benefits, barriers and ways to reduce exclusion.", "points": ["Convenience and speed benefit", "Connectivity and device barriers", "Digital literacy and accessibility barriers", "Language and usability barriers", "Practical inclusive mitigation"], "answer": "Making a government service fully digital may improve speed and convenience, but it is not automatically social progress. A service is socially beneficial only when its advantages are available to the people who depend on it.\n\nThe lecture identifies several barriers. Rural or underserved users may have poor connectivity. Some people may have limited digital literacy, while others may lack a suitable device. People with disabilities may need accessible interfaces, and language or usability problems may prevent eligible users from completing the service. A digital-only approach may therefore improve the experience for confident internet users while making access more difficult for vulnerable groups.\n\nA responsible implementation should include accessibility testing, clearer language, user support and workable alternatives for people who cannot access the digital channel. The engineer should assess who benefits, who is excluded and how the burden can be reduced. Digital transformation becomes meaningful progress when it improves services without unfairly leaving people behind."}, {"topic": "Social responsibility", "kind": "Apply", "marks": 12, "slides": "29–30", "question": "Create a stakeholder impact map for the 92%-accurate AI student-risk system, including three stakeholders and an appropriate responsibility for each.", "points": ["Identify at least three distinct stakeholders", "Benefit to each", "Risk or concern to each", "Responsible action for each", "Overall accountable decision"], "answer": "The first stakeholder is the student. The potential benefit is earlier academic support, but the student may be falsely labelled, experience stigma or lose privacy. The university should explain how the prediction is used, protect student information and provide a way to question incorrect decisions.\n\nThe second stakeholder is the lecturer. The prediction may help lecturers identify students needing assistance, but an inaccurate label could influence the lecturer’s expectations or treatment of a student. Lecturers should use the result carefully, avoid treating it as a final judgement and consider the student’s circumstances.\n\nThe third stakeholder is university management. The system may support planning and allocation of student-support resources, but its decisions can create institutional unfairness or reputational harm. Management should be accountable for oversight, reviewing model limitations and ensuring that the system serves students’ interests.\n\nThe overall decision should be based on demonstrated benefit, fairness and effective safeguards, not on the 92% figure alone. This follows the stakeholder map’s questions about benefits, harm, risk, accountability and mitigation."}, {"topic": "Social responsibility", "kind": "Analyse", "marks": 10, "slides": "28–31", "question": "Explain how software can create positive social impact while also producing negative indirect consequences.", "points": ["At least three positive areas from lecture", "Direct and indirect effects", "Example with unintended disadvantage", "Safeguards to reduce harms"], "answer": "Software can improve people’s lives through better accessibility, healthcare, education, disaster response, resource efficiency, financial inclusion and community services. These benefits can arise when a software feature removes an existing barrier or helps resources reach people more effectively.\n\nHowever, beneficial software can also produce indirect harm. For example, an online learning platform may expand educational access for students with good internet connections but disadvantage those who have weak connectivity or unsuitable devices. Direct users may report an improved experience even while other students become less able to participate. The ripple effect means that engineers must consider organisations, communities and other stakeholders beyond their initial target users.\n\nA responsible approach is to identify intended benefits alongside potential exclusion, consult affected groups and test whether the proposed software actually reduces the problem. Positive intentions do not replace evidence and consideration of unintended consequences."}, {"topic": "SDGs and priorities", "kind": "Explain", "marks": 10, "slides": "32", "question": "What are the SDGs? Explain how software could contribute to four SDGs highlighted in the lecture.", "points": ["17 UN goals under 2030 Agenda", "Correctly name four slide-listed SDGs", "Give a plausible software mechanism for each", "Avoid automatic impact claims"], "answer": "The United Nations Sustainable Development Goals are 17 global goals under the 2030 Agenda. They provide a way to connect software development with wider social, economic and environmental problems.\n\nSoftware may support SDG 3, Good Health and Well-being, by helping people access a healthcare service more effectively. It may support SDG 4, Quality Education, by improving access to learning resources. SDG 9, Industry, Innovation and Infrastructure, may be supported when software strengthens useful digital infrastructure or industrial capability. SDG 10, Reduced Inequalities, may be supported when a service is designed to reduce barriers faced by underserved users.\n\nThe lecture also highlights SDGs 11, 12 and 13. The important point is that an SDG connection must be explained, not merely stated. An engineer should identify the actual software function, show how it addresses a real problem, and consider evidence of the claimed improvement."}, {"topic": "SDGs and priorities", "kind": "Analyse", "marks": 12, "slides": "33–34", "question": "A smart waste platform monitors bin capacity, predicts demand and optimises routes. Explain its contribution to SDGs 11, 12 and 13, including how the contributions could be demonstrated.", "points": ["Describe system functions from lecture", "SDG 11 mechanism", "SDG 12 mechanism", "SDG 13 mechanism", "Distinguish expected outcome from evidence"], "answer": "The smart waste platform uses IoT sensors to monitor bin capacity, predicts collection demand, optimises collection routes, provides recycling information and analyses waste patterns. Its social value depends on how these functions improve waste management rather than simply on using IoT.\n\nFor SDG 11, Sustainable Cities and Communities, timely collection and better route planning may support cleaner, better-managed local areas. For SDG 12, Responsible Consumption and Production, recycling information and analysis of waste patterns may help communities and service operators use resources more responsibly. For SDG 13, Climate Action, eliminating unnecessary collection journeys may reduce fuel consumption and associated emissions.\n\nThese are plausible contribution mechanisms, not guaranteed results. The project should compare actual operational outcomes with an appropriate baseline. For example, the team could examine collection efficiency, recycling participation and transport fuel use before and after implementation. This follows the lecture’s instruction to explain exactly how a project contributes to an SDG."}, {"topic": "SDGs and priorities", "kind": "Explain", "marks": 8, "slides": "35", "question": "Explain the purpose of the 10-10 MySTIE Framework and how a software project could relate to it.", "points": ["Name MySTIE correctly", "10 socioeconomic and 10 science/technology drivers", "Align innovation with Malaysian needs", "Concrete aligned software illustration", "Do not invent named drivers"], "answer": "The 10-10 MySTIE Framework is presented in the lecture as a way to connect technology with Malaysia’s socioeconomic needs and opportunities. Its structure links ten key socioeconomic drivers with ten science and technology drivers. The central idea is that scientific and technological innovation should respond to real national needs rather than exist only as a technical exercise.\n\nFor example, a smart waste-management project could use software, sensors and data analysis to respond to the practical need for better public services and resource use. The project team would first identify the relevant social or economic problem, then explain how its chosen technology could help address it.\n\nThe lecture does not list the individual MySTIE drivers, so an exam answer based only on these slides should not invent or claim specific driver names. The supported takeaway is the need for alignment between innovation and socioeconomic value."}, {"topic": "SDGs and priorities", "kind": "Explain", "marks": 8, "slides": "36", "question": "What is MyDIGITAL, and which themes from the lecture are relevant to responsible software development?", "points": ["Malaysia Digital Economy Blueprint", "Seven broad lecture themes", "Explain at least three themes in context", "Include inclusion/cybersecurity"], "answer": "MyDIGITAL is the Malaysia Digital Economy Blueprint. In the lecture, it provides a national context for how software engineering contributes to Malaysia’s digital economy. The seven broad themes are digital services, digital adoption, data, cybersecurity, digital talent, inclusion and innovation.\n\nA software engineer may contribute to digital services by building useful online systems, but responsible development also requires addressing cybersecurity so users can trust those services. Digital adoption depends on whether people and organisations are capable of using the solution, while inclusion asks whether different groups can benefit from it. Attention to data and digital talent supports responsible implementation and continuing capability.\n\nTherefore, aligning a project with MyDIGITAL should not mean simply calling it “digital”. The developer should explain how the solution creates a useful service and consider whether it can be adopted safely and inclusively."}, {"topic": "SDGs and priorities", "kind": "Compare", "marks": 10, "slides": "35–38", "question": "Compare the roles of SDGs, MySTIE, MyDIGITAL and Industry4WRD when justifying a software project.", "points": ["SDGs as global societal goals", "MySTIE as national socioeconomic/technology alignment", "MyDIGITAL as digital-economy themes", "Industry4WRD as industry priority named but not detailed", "Integrate in one example"], "answer": "The SDGs provide a global framework for explaining which societal or environmental problem a project addresses. MySTIE asks how science, technology and innovation can connect with Malaysia’s socioeconomic needs. MyDIGITAL provides the national digital-economy context, including digital services, adoption, data, cybersecurity, talent, inclusion and innovation. Industry4WRD is identified in the lecture as a Malaysian industry-related priority, although its detailed components are not described.\n\nFor example, a smart waste platform could be connected to SDGs 11, 12 and 13 through better collection, resource use and possibly lower emissions. Its use of data and software to respond to an identified national need reflects the MySTIE idea. Reliable digital services and responsible data handling relate to MyDIGITAL. If the system is implemented in an industrial operating context, Industry4WRD may be relevant as a broader industry-facing priority.\n\nThese are complementary perspectives, not interchangeable labels. A strong answer explains the concrete link to each rather than assuming every software project supports all frameworks automatically."}, {"topic": "SDGs and priorities", "kind": "Evaluate", "marks": 12, "slides": "37–38", "question": "Apply the lecture’s four perspectives—ethics, social responsibility, SDGs and Malaysian priorities—to the smart waste-management system.", "points": ["Ethical responsibility and potential risk", "Stakeholders and wider effects", "Correct SDGs", "MySTIE and MyDIGITAL links", "Careful Industry4WRD claim", "Evidence or evaluation"], "answer": "From an ethics perspective, the smart waste platform should be evaluated for reliability, honest reporting and responsible handling of the information it collects. A failed prediction or poorly designed route could create operational problems, so benefits should not be assumed without testing.\n\nFrom a social-responsibility perspective, the system affects residents, waste-collection employees, service operators, local communities and potentially the environment. Better collection may benefit neighbourhoods, but the project should examine whether some areas or groups are underserved.\n\nFor SDGs, improved urban waste services relate to SDG 11. Recycling support and waste-pattern analysis relate to SDG 12, while more efficient routes may support SDG 13 if they actually reduce emissions. In Malaysia, the project reflects MySTIE’s alignment of innovation with socioeconomic needs and MyDIGITAL’s themes of digital services, data and innovation. Industry4WRD may provide a relevant industrial context, but the lecture gives no detailed criteria for claiming specific Industry4WRD outcomes.\n\nThe project’s impact should be tested using actual service and environmental results, not just the presence of technical features."}, {"topic": "Synthesis", "kind": "Evaluate", "marks": 10, "slides": "39–42", "question": "“A software system can be technically successful and still create social harm.” Discuss this statement and explain what an engineer should do.", "points": ["Explain gap between functionality and impact", "Mention users and non-users", "Risk, fairness and transparency", "Professional accountability and safeguards", "Three-part takeaway"], "answer": "I agree with the statement because software can perform its intended functions while creating harmful consequences for users or society. A system may achieve high accuracy, automate work efficiently or reduce costs, yet still collect unnecessary data, treat some groups unfairly or exclude people who cannot use it.\n\nThe lecture’s student-risk AI illustrates the problem. Even with 92% accuracy, incorrect labels may cause stigma or change the way lecturers treat students. The engineer should identify everyone affected, assess benefits and risks, communicate limitations honestly and ensure there is accountability for harmful outcomes. Where possible, the team should redesign the system or introduce safeguards, such as ways to correct errors and review important decisions.\n\nUltimately, responsible software engineers must build it right through quality, safety and reliability; build the right thing by understanding people and consequences; and think beyond the system by considering society, sustainability and the wider context."}, {"topic": "Synthesis", "kind": "Propose", "marks": 12, "slides": "28, 31–38", "question": "Propose a software solution that addresses a Malaysian societal problem, and explain the ethics, stakeholders, SDG and national-priority connections.", "points": ["Define one specific social problem", "Concrete software functions", "Direct and invisible stakeholders", "At least two ethical risks/mitigations", "Relevant SDG with mechanism", "MySTIE and/or MyDIGITAL connection"], "answer": "I propose an accessible community flood-alert application to help residents receive timely information about local flood risks. It could display alerts, provide safety instructions and allow community officers to communicate updates. The intended benefit is to make important information easier to receive and act upon during an emergency.\n\nDirect stakeholders include residents and local response teams. Indirect stakeholders include family members, nearby communities and people who cannot access smartphones. Ethical risks include inaccurate alerts, privacy concerns if location information is used, and the exclusion of people with limited connectivity or accessibility needs. The design should make uncertainty clear, protect necessary data and provide alternative notification methods where feasible.\n\nThe project could contribute to SDG 11, Sustainable Cities and Communities, through improved community preparedness. Its use of software to address a practical national need reflects the MySTIE principle. As a digital public-facing service, it also relates to MyDIGITAL themes such as digital services, inclusion and cybersecurity.\n\nThe team should evaluate whether alerts reach intended users in a timely, understandable and inclusive manner before claiming success."}], "mcq": [{"question": "A university AI predicts student dropout risk with 92% accuracy. What is the strongest ethical conclusion?", "choices": ["Accuracy proves it is fair", "Deploy immediately because it works", "Evaluate privacy, bias and unintended harms before deciding", "Students have no right to question the model"], "correct": 2, "explanation": "Accuracy measures one aspect of technical performance, not fairness, consent, social impact or accountability.", "slides": "3–4"}, {"question": "Which question examines purpose, need and ethical concerns?", "choices": ["Can we build it?", "Should we build it?", "How quickly can we ship it?", "What language should we use?"], "correct": 1, "explanation": "“Should we build it?” evaluates ethical justification, rather than technical feasibility.", "slides": "5"}, {"question": "Which option best describes ethics in software engineering?", "choices": ["Only following company policy", "Only complying with law", "Reasoning about responsible, fair and appropriate actions affecting others", "Building software that customers will pay for"], "correct": 2, "explanation": "The slide defines ethics through responsible, fair and appropriate judgement.", "slides": "7–8"}, {"question": "A user clicks “I Agree” to unnecessary microphone and contacts permissions. What follows?", "choices": ["The engineer has no further duty", "Consent alone makes data collection ethical", "Only the marketing team is accountable", "The engineer must still question necessity and impact"], "correct": 3, "explanation": "User agreement does not automatically eliminate professional ethical responsibility.", "slides": "9"}, {"question": "Which ACM/IEEE area asks whether software is fit, safe and reliable?", "choices": ["Product", "Profession", "Colleagues", "Self"], "correct": 0, "explanation": "Area 3 (Product) concerns product suitability, safety, security and reliability.", "slides": "11, 14"}, {"question": "An engineer refuses to approve an unsupported “100% secure” claim. Which area is most directly relevant?", "choices": ["Management", "Profession", "Colleagues", "Self"], "correct": 1, "explanation": "Area 6 includes honest claims and the integrity of the profession.", "slides": "17"}, {"question": "Which ethical trade-off most directly concerns automated AI decisions that cannot be challenged?", "choices": ["Cost vs quality", "Convenience vs privacy", "Automation vs human control", "Speed vs safety"], "correct": 2, "explanation": "Automation can reduce opportunities for human review and control.", "slides": "20"}, {"question": "What is the first question in the lecture’s simple ethical decision check?", "choices": ["Who could be affected?", "Can we deliver tomorrow?", "Will management approve?", "What programming language is preferred?"], "correct": 0, "explanation": "The checklist begins by identifying affected parties.", "slides": "21"}, {"question": "An individual never uses an app but is negatively affected by decisions made using it. They are best described as:", "choices": ["A direct user only", "An invisible or indirect stakeholder", "A software tester", "An irrelevant outsider"], "correct": 1, "explanation": "The lecture explicitly considers people harmed or helped without directly using the system.", "slides": "25–26"}, {"question": "Why is a 100% digital government service not automatically social progress?", "choices": ["Digital services are always insecure", "Physical services must always be faster", "Every user has a suitable device", "Connectivity, literacy, accessibility and language barriers may exclude people"], "correct": 3, "explanation": "The slide highlights several inclusion barriers, despite gains in convenience.", "slides": "27"}, {"question": "How many Sustainable Development Goals are in the UN 2030 Agenda?", "choices": ["7", "10", "17", "20"], "correct": 2, "explanation": "The SDG framework contains 17 global goals; the lecture highlights seven.", "slides": "32"}, {"question": "Which SDGs are explicitly connected to the smart waste-management case?", "choices": ["SDGs 3, 4 and 9", "SDGs 11, 12 and 13", "SDGs 1, 2 and 5", "SDGs 6, 8 and 16"], "correct": 1, "explanation": "The lecture highlights sustainable communities, responsible consumption and climate action.", "slides": "33–34"}, {"question": "The 10-10 MySTIE Framework connects:", "choices": ["Ten universities to ten private firms", "Ten SDGs to ten digital services", "Ten socioeconomic drivers to ten science-and-technology drivers", "Ten data centres to ten networks"], "correct": 2, "explanation": "MySTIE relates national socioeconomic needs to science, technology and innovation.", "slides": "35"}, {"question": "Which is NOT a theme explicitly listed under MyDIGITAL in the lecture?", "choices": ["Cybersecurity", "Inclusion", "Digital talent", "100% guaranteed AI accuracy"], "correct": 3, "explanation": "The seven listed themes are digital services, adoption, data, cybersecurity, talent, inclusion and innovation.", "slides": "36"}, {"question": "The lecture’s three final messages are:", "choices": ["Code, test, ship", "Build it right; build the right thing; think beyond the system", "Automate everything; minimise costs; protect profits", "Use AI; adopt cloud; replace manual systems"], "correct": 1, "explanation": "These are the exact concluding principles on slides 40–41.", "slides": "40–41"}], "flashcards": [{"front": "What are the three main engineering questions?", "back": "Can we build it? Should we build it? What could happen?", "slides": "5"}, {"front": "What does “Can we build it?” assess?", "back": "Technical feasibility, technology and resources.", "slides": "5"}, {"front": "What does “Should we build it?” assess?", "back": "Purpose, need and ethical concerns.", "slides": "5"}, {"front": "Why is 92% accuracy not enough?", "back": "It does not prove fairness, privacy, accountability or lack of harm.", "slides": "3–4"}, {"front": "What is ethics in this lecture?", "back": "Reasoning about responsible, fair and appropriate decisions affecting others.", "slides": "7"}, {"front": "Name the six ethical considerations.", "back": "Harm/benefit, fairness, privacy, safety, honesty and accountability.", "slides": "7"}, {"front": "What is the difference between law and ethics?", "back": "Law sets permissions/duties; ethics asks what responsible judgement requires.", "slides": "8"}, {"front": "Does clicking “I Agree” end ethical responsibility?", "back": "No. Necessity, informed choice and effects on others still matter.", "slides": "9"}, {"front": "What is the purpose of a professional code?", "back": "To guide judgement when technical, business and social interests conflict.", "slides": "10"}, {"front": "List the eight ACM/IEEE areas in order.", "back": "Public; Client & Employer; Product; Judgment; Management; Profession; Colleagues; Self.", "slides": "11"}, {"front": "What is ACM/IEEE Area 1?", "back": "Public: consider harm, exclusion, failures and honest risk communication.", "slides": "12"}, {"front": "What is ACM/IEEE Area 2?", "back": "Client & Employer: serve them responsibly, consistent with public interest.", "slides": "13"}, {"front": "What is ACM/IEEE Area 3?", "back": "Product: ensure fit, safety, security, reliability and error recovery.", "slides": "14"}, {"front": "What is ACM/IEEE Area 4?", "back": "Judgment: maintain objective professional judgement under pressure.", "slides": "15"}, {"front": "What is ACM/IEEE Area 5?", "back": "Management: realistic plans, fair workloads, honest reporting and quality.", "slides": "16"}, {"front": "What is ACM/IEEE Area 6?", "back": "Profession: uphold integrity and avoid misleading software claims.", "slides": "17"}, {"front": "What is ACM/IEEE Area 7?", "back": "Colleagues: give credit, support learning and avoid unfair blame.", "slides": "18"}, {"front": "What is ACM/IEEE Area 8?", "back": "Self: keep learning, know your limits and ask for help.", "slides": "19"}, {"front": "Name two trade-offs involving privacy.", "back": "Convenience vs privacy; personalisation vs data collection.", "slides": "20"}, {"front": "What is the automation trade-off?", "back": "Automation vs human control.", "slides": "20"}, {"front": "What are ethical-check questions 1–4?", "back": "Who is affected? What can go wrong? Who benefits? Who carries risk?", "slides": "21"}, {"front": "What are ethical-check questions 5–7?", "back": "Do users understand? Safer/fairer alternative? Can it be defended?", "slides": "21"}, {"front": "How do ethics and social responsibility differ?", "back": "Ethics asks what is responsible; social responsibility asks who is affected.", "slides": "23"}, {"front": "What is the ripple effect of software?", "back": "Direct users → organisation → other stakeholders → community → society/environment.", "slides": "24"}, {"front": "What is an invisible stakeholder?", "back": "Someone affected by software despite not directly using it.", "slides": "26"}, {"front": "Name two barriers to digital-only public services.", "back": "Examples: poor connectivity, low digital literacy or inaccessible design.", "slides": "27"}, {"front": "What are five stakeholder-impact map prompts?", "back": "Benefits, harms, failures, accountability and mitigation.", "slides": "29"}, {"front": "How many UN SDGs are there?", "back": "17 global goals in the 2030 Agenda.", "slides": "32"}, {"front": "Which SDGs are linked to smart waste?", "back": "SDG 11, SDG 12 and SDG 13.", "slides": "34"}, {"front": "What is the lesson of the smart waste SDG case?", "back": "Explain the actual mechanism and seek evidence of contribution.", "slides": "34"}, {"front": "What is 10-10 MySTIE?", "back": "Ten socioeconomic drivers linked to ten science and technology drivers.", "slides": "35"}, {"front": "Name the MyDIGITAL themes.", "back": "Services, adoption, data, cybersecurity, talent, inclusion, innovation.", "slides": "36"}, {"front": "What does the lecture establish about Industry4WRD?", "back": "It names an industry-facing Malaysian priority; details are not supplied.", "slides": "37–38"}, {"front": "What are the four final project perspectives?", "back": "Ethics, social responsibility, SDGs and Malaysian priorities.", "slides": "38"}, {"front": "What are the three final engineering takeaways?", "back": "Build it right; build the right thing; think beyond the system.", "slides": "40–41"}]};
const STORAGE_PREFIX='ethics_v2_';
